ss: { emailAddress: \"j@x.com\" } },\n      \"fallback-id\",\n    )).toBe(\"j@x.com\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when user is null\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel(null, \"fallback-id\")).toBe(\"fallback-id\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"falls back to the stable id when both username and email are absent\", () => {\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: null, primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"fallback-id\"))\n      .toBe(\"fallback-id\");\n  });\n\n  it(\"treats empty-string username as absent, not as a valid label\", () => {\n    // current implementation uses ?? — an empty string WOULD pass through.\n    // this test documents/locks the intended behavior once decided.\n    expect(reviewerLabel({ username: \"\", primaryEmailAddress: null }, \"fallback-id\"))\n      .toBe(\"fallback-id\"); // currently FAILS — flags a real edge case gap\n  });\n});\n```\nNote: that last case is a genuine bug-shaped gap — `??` does not treat `\"\"` as nullish, so an empty-string Clerk username would render as a blank label in the audit trail rather than falling back. Worth deciding intentionally.\n\n### `lib/cn.ts`\n```ts\n// web/lib/cn.test.ts\ndescribe(\"cn\", () => {\n  it(\"merges conflicting tailwind classes, last one wins\", () => {\n    expect(cn(\"px-2\", \"px-4\")).toBe(\"px-4\");\n  });\n  it(\"drops falsy values\", () => {\n    expect(cn(\"a\", false, undefined, null, \"b\")).toBe(\"a b\");\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### `types/source.ts` — Zod schemas, the actual API-boundary validation\n```ts\n// web/types/source.test.ts\nimport { createSourceSchema, sourceConfigSchema } from \"@/types/source\";\n\ndescribe(\"createSourceSchema\", () => {\n  it(\"rejects an empty name\", () => {\n    expect(createSourceSchema.safeParse({ name: \"\", kind: \"synthetic\", config: { objects: [\"a\"] } }).success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects a name over 80 chars\", () => {\n    expect(createSourceSchema.safeParse({ name: \"x\".repeat(81), kind: \"synthetic\", config: { objects: [\"a\"] } }).success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects an unknown kind\", () => {\n    expect(createSourceSchema.safeParse({ name: \"n\", kind: \"bigquery\", config: { objects: [\"a\"] } }).success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects config with zero objects\", () => {\n    expect(sourceConfigSchema.safeParse({ objects: [] }).success).toBe(false);\n  });\n  it(\"rejects a config containing a credential-shaped key\", () => {\n    // .extend/.strict isn't set — schema currently allows arbitrary extra keys\n    // through unless `.strict()`. This is exactly the shape a secret could hide in.\n    const result = sourceConfigSchema.safeParse({ objects: [\"a\"], password: \"hunter2\" });\n    expect(result.success).toBe(true); // documents a real gap: nothing stops a\n                                        // caller from putting a secret in config\n  });\n});\n```\nThat last test is the most important one in this file — `AGENTS.md`'s security table is explicit that `sources.config` must never hold credentials, but `sourceConfigSchema` has no `.strict()` and no denylist, so nothing currently enforces it at the validation boundary.\n\n### `lib/server/sources.ts` — `listSources`, `countSourcesBySide` (needs DB mocking)\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.test.ts\nvi.mock(\"@/lib/db\", () => ({ getDb: vi.fn() }));\n\ndescribe(\"listSources\", () => {\n  it(\"parses every row through sourceSchema and throws on a malformed row\", async () => {\n    // a row missing a required field should fail loudly, not silently coerce\n    ...\n  });\n  it(\"returns an empty array when no sources are configured\", async () => {\n    ...\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"countSourcesBySide\", () => {\n  it(\"classifies salesforce and hubspot as crm\", () => { ... });\n  it(\"classifies databricks and snowflake as warehouse\", () => { ... });\n  it(\"classifies synthetic separately, not counted in crm or warehouse\", () => { ... });\n  it(\"returns all zeros when listSources is empty\", () => { ... });\n});\n```\n\n### `lib/server/auth.ts` — `requireReviewer`, `getReviewer` (error-handling path currently untested)\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/auth.test.ts\nvi.mock(\"@clerk/nextjs/server\");\n\ndescribe(\"requireReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"throws when there is no authenticated session\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(auth).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false, userId: null } as any);\n    await expect(requireReviewer()).rejects.toThrow(/No authenticated reviewer/);\n  });\n\n  it(\"returns userId and a resolved label when authenticated\", async () => {\n    ...\n  });\n});\n\ndescribe(\"getReviewer\", () => {\n  it(\"returns null instead of throwing when signed out\", async () => {\n    vi.mocked(auth).mockResolvedValue({ isAuthenticated: false } as any);\n    await expect(getReviewer()).resolves.toBeNull();\n  });\n});\n```\n\n### Components — `SeverityMark`, `SourceTable` (render/a11y contract untested)\n`AGENTS.md` hard-requires the mark is \"never rendered without the word\" — that's a testable accessibility contract today, not just a style guideline.\n\n```tsx\n// web/components/severity-mark.test.tsx\nimport { render, screen } from \"@testing-library/react\";\n\ndescribe(\"SeverityMark\", () => {\n  it.each([1, 2, 3, 4] as const)(\"severity %i always renders its text label alongside the mark\", (sev) => {\n    render(<SeverityMark severity={sev} />);\n    expect(screen.getByText(SEVERITY_LABEL[sev])).toBeInTheDocument();\n  });\n});\n\n// web/components/sources/source-table.test.tsx\ndescribe(\"SourceTable\", () => {\n  it(\"renders one row per source\", () => { ... });\n  it(\"renders an empty tbody, not a crash, for zero sources\", () => { ... });\n  it(\"joins multiple config.objects with a comma\", () => { ... });\n});\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Integration test gaps (the biggest structural hole)\n\nNone of these exist yet, and per `AGENTS.md` they're where the layer boundary actually gets enforced:\n\n| Gap | Why it matters | Sketch |\n|---|---|---|\n| No 